Goal:
Create a mechanism to update Slurm job priority weights and simulate it to determine if it can be effective at adjusting to changing workloads and providing good service.

Project Constraints:
Only work inside the ver_0.3 folder and environment. Do not reference any folder outside of ver_0.3 in the repository.

Setup:

Cluster:
Nodes: 16 nodes, n01–n16, each with 32 cores (2 sockets × 16) and 128 GB of memory (RealMemory=128000 MB). That's 512 cores and 2 TB in total, with no GPUs.
Partition: one partition, normal, with one QOS and a 7-day MaxTime
Scheduler: Slurm default
Priority: Recomputed every minute, can be tuned later

Workload Data: Workloads from the real trace in `dataset/`: limit it to one week at first. Scale later.

Simulator:
UB Slurm Simulator


JOB CLASSIFICATION
Classify jobs into 9 types based on their requested resources and time limits

SMALL
1) sm-short: small resource requirements - short time limit
2) sm-med: small resource requirements - medium time limit
3) sm-long: small resource requirements - long time limit

MEDIUM
4) med-short: medium resource requirements - short time limit
5) med-med: medium resource requirements - medium time limit
6) med-long: medium resource requirements - long time limit

LARGE
4) lg-short: large resource requirements - short time limit
5) lg-med: large resource requirements - medium time limit
6) lg-long: large resource requirements - long time limit


WEIGHT CONFIGURATIONS
Create job weight configurations based on the built in priority weights in Slurm. They will prioritize certain metrics to achieve different goals

1) Fairness > Utilization > Load Balance
2) Fairness > Load Balance > Utilization
3) Load Balance > Fairness > Utilization
4) Load Balance > Utilization > Fairness
5) Utilization > Fairness > Load Balance
6) Utilization > Load Balance > Fairness

Definitions:

Fairness: A goal to make sure that no set of users gets priority of job placement on the cluster over others. Slurm's PriorityWeightFairshare x fairshare helps enforce this.
Utilization: A goal to try to utilize as much of a node as possible - both cpu and memory.
Load Balance: A goal to try and keep utilization even across all nodes.



Work Steps:
1) Create workloads from trace data that fit the cluster
2) Classify jobs using JOB CLASSIFICATION descriptions
3) Create priority weight configurations using WEIGHT CONFIGURATIONS descriptions
4) Determine cluster states that trigger a configuration change
5) Create configuration change mechanism
6) Simulate workloads with the full setup


Phases:
1) Steps 1-3
2) Steps 4-5
3) Step 6
